          guideText: `{name}, Latin America is roughly 660 million people across 33 countries and dependencies, with a complex layered history: Indigenous civilizations (Maya, Aztec, Inca, and many others) over thousands of years before European arrival; Spanish and Portuguese colonization 1492 onward producing the most intensive colonial extraction system in history; independence wars early 19th century producing the contemporary state borders; multiple cycles of revolution, dictatorship, democratic transition, and contested present. The patterns parallel the African post-colonial trajectories you engaged in L15: extractive economic structures imposed by European colonization; Cold War interventions (substantially US-backed, covered in L11); resource curse dynamics; varied transitions to democracy with continuing struggles. The patterns also have distinctively Latin American features: deep racial mixing and complex racial categories (mestizo, mulato, criollo, indigenous); Catholic religious dominance with substantial Indigenous-Catholic syncretism; left-revolutionary tradition more developed and sustained than most of the world; rich cultural production that has shaped global culture substantially. Three case studies: Mexico (longest sustained left-revolutionary state, complex one-party system, current López Obrador / Sheinbaum trajectory); Cuba (1959 revolution as foundational reference point for hemisphere; 60+ years sustained Communist rule; current transition under economic crisis); Chile and Argentina together (Cold War dictatorship to democratic transition with continuing reckoning, illustrating the broader pattern most directly).`,
