              definition: `Latin American culture substantially shapes global culture. Six dimensions. Literature: "Boom" of 1960s-70s (Gabriel García Márquez, Colombian, "One Hundred Years of Solitude" 1967, Nobel 1982, magical realism); Jorge Luis Borges (Argentine, "Ficciones" 1944); Mario Vargas Llosa (Peruvian-Spanish, Nobel 2010, "perfect dictatorship" phrase for PRI); Julio Cortázar; Carlos Fuentes; Pablo Neruda (Chilean, Nobel 1971); Octavio Paz (Nobel 1990); Sor Juana Inés de la Cruz (1648-1695); Rubén Darío (modernismo); Roberto Bolaño "2666"; Isabel Allende; Junot Díaz; Valeria Luiselli; Yuri Herrera; Samanta Schweblin. Music: Cuban son + salsa influenced jazz; Brazilian samba + bossa nova (João Gilberto, Tom Jobim, Vinícius de Moraes from 1958+) + tropicália (Caetano Veloso, Gilberto Gil, Gal Costa from 1967+); Argentine/Uruguayan tango; Mexican ranchera/mariachi/corridos; reggaeton (Puerto Rico + Panama 1990s+) globally dominant by 2010s; Bad Bunny most-streamed Spotify artist globally multiple years; Shakira (Colombian); J Balvin/Maluma/Karol G (Colombian). Cinema: Argentine ("Official Story" 1985, "Secret in Their Eyes" 2009; Lucrecia Martel); Mexican "Three Amigos" Alejandro González Iñárritu + Alfonso Cuarón + Guillermo del Toro winning 4 of last 11 Best Director Oscars (Iñárritu 2014, 2015; Cuarón 2013, 2018; del Toro 2017); Cuarón "Roma" 2018; del Toro "Shape of Water" Best Picture 2017; Brazilian Glauber Rocha Cinema Novo 1960s-70s + Fernando Meirelles "City of God" 2002. Visual art: Frida Kahlo (1907-1954) globally foundational; Diego Rivera (1886-1957); Mexican muralism (Rivera, José Clemente Orozco, David Alfaro Siqueiros); Fernando Botero (Colombian, 1932-2023); Roberto Matta (Chilean surrealist 1911-2002); Tania Bruguera (Cuban); Doris Salcedo (Colombian); Vik Muniz (Brazilian). Religion: ~70% Catholic historically (substantial Protestant evangelical growth in Brazil + Central America); Indigenous-Catholic syncretism (Day of the Dead, Andean Pachamama-Catholic, Afro-Brazilian Candomblé + Umbanda); liberation theology Gustavo Gutiérrez "A Theology of Liberation" 1971; Pope Francis (Argentine, first Latin American pope, since 2013). Political/intellectual: Eduardo Galeano (Uruguayan) "Open Veins of Latin America" 1971 foundational anti-imperialist; Paulo Freire (Brazilian) "Pedagogy of the Oppressed" 1968 globally influential education theory; Walter Mignolo + Aníbal Quijano (decolonial thought); Enrique Dussel (liberation philosophy). Current left wave: López Obrador + Sheinbaum Mexico; Gustavo Petro Colombia (first leftist president, took office 2022); Gabriel Boric Chile (2022); Lula Brazil (returned 2023); Pedro Castillo Peru (2021-2022) + Dina Boluarte after removal; Maduro Venezuela.`,
              audioPrompt: `Latin American cultural production has substantially shaped global culture, {name}. Literature produced one of the 20th century's most influential movements: the "Boom" of the 1960s and 1970s. Major figures include Gabriel García Márquez, the Colombian who wrote "One Hundred Years of Solitude" in 1967 and won the Nobel Prize in 1982; magical realism became the dominant Latin American literary mode. Jorge Luis Borges, the Argentine. Mario Vargas Llosa, Peruvian-Spanish, Nobel 2010. Pablo Neruda, Chilean poet, Nobel 1971. Octavio Paz, Mexican, Nobel 1990. Music: Cuban son and salsa influenced jazz development. Brazilian samba and bossa nova starting from 1958 onward. Argentine and Uruguayan tango. Reggaeton originating in Puerto Rico and Panama in the 1990s became globally dominant by the 2010s; Bad Bunny became the most-streamed artist on Spotify globally for multiple years. Cinema: Mexican "Three Amigos" (Alejandro González Iñárritu, Alfonso Cuarón, and Guillermo del Toro) have won 4 of the last 11 Best Director Oscars. Visual art: Frida Kahlo, 1907 to 1954, globally recognized as one of the 20th century's most important artists. Diego Rivera's Mexican muralism. Religion: liberation theology emerged from Latin American Catholicism in the 1960s and 1970s; Pope Francis since 2013 is the first Latin American pope.`,
